  <Section tone="paper" padding="default" id="engineering" class="scroll-mt-24">
    <Container width="content">
      <div class="case-layout">
        <div class="case-layout__head">
          <Heading level={2} visual="display-md" tone="ink">Engineering</Heading>
        </div>
        <div class="case-layout__body">
          <Heading level={3} visual="h3" tone="ink" class="mt-8 mb-3">Accessibility comes from React Aria, styling comes from tokens</Heading>
          <div class="measure space-y-5 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted text-lg">
            <p>
              Interactive components are built on React Aria hooks, so keyboard navigation, focus
              management, ARIA roles and pointer handling come from a library maintained by Adobe.
              Rudiment owns the look and the API. That’s why buttons take <code>onPress</code> rather
              than <code>onClick</code>, and why <code>onChange</code> hands back a value instead of an
              event.
            </p>
            <p>
              Every component has a test file that checks rendering, keyboard and pointer use, and its
              disabled, loading and error states, and runs axe against the result. Tests find elements
              by role and text, never by class name, so restyling a component can’t quietly break its
              tests. A second test project renders every story in a real Chromium browser with the
              Storybook accessibility addon.
            </p>
          </div>

          <Heading level={3} visual="h3" tone="ink" class="mt-12 mb-3">A package that doesn’t fight the app it’s installed in</Heading>
          <div class="measure space-y-5 leading-relaxed text-ink-muted text-lg">
            <p>
              Storybook uses Tailwind for its own pages, but the published package doesn’t include it.
              Components use BEM class names like <code>rudi-button--primary</code>, and the stylesheet
              has no reset and no bare element selectors, so installing Rudiment can’t restyle the rest
              of an app. The build keeps React Aria as an external dependency, because two copies of it
              break its context, and adds a <code>'use client'</code> banner so the package can be
              imported straight into a Next.js Server Component.
            </p>
          </div>

          <div class="mt-10 rounded-lg border border-line p-6 md:p-8">
            <p class="mono-label mb-6">Stack</p>
            <ul class="grid gap-x-8 gap-y-5 sm:grid-cols-2">
              {stack.map((item) => (
                <li>
                  <p class="font-medium text-ink leading-snug">{item.name}</p>
                  <p class="mt-0.5 text-sm text-ink-muted leading-snug">{item.role}</p>
                </li>
              ))}
            </ul>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Container>
  </Section>

